<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oghuz Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oghuz Kagan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oghuz Khan is the legendary ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, celebrated in Turkic epic and genealogical tradition. Stories of his conquests and of his twenty-four grandsons, the ancestors of the Oghuz tribes, are told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> Oghuzname and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a>'s history. Dynasties including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> claimed descent from him through these tribes, and he is a national hero in modern Turkmenistan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan.jpg" alt="Oghuz Khan" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jami al-Tawarikh artist, 1420-1430 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oghuz_Khan_enthroned,_folio_9r_(Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale,_Suppl%C3%A9ment_persan_1113).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
